{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And YHWH spoke to Moses on Mount Sinai, saying.</b> What has the matter of the sabbatical year to do with Mount Sinai — and so forth? It is nearly to be heard that when Moses ascended Mount Sinai after the seven weeks that Israel counted — forty-nine days from Pesah to Atzeret — that mountain was then sanctified and became restricted against sowing and plowing on the fiftieth day, on which the Torah was given. And that is the time of the drawing of the Jubilee, to proclaim freedom for all Israel — freedom inscribed on the Tablets — and by means of the sound of the shofar of the giving of the Torah. At that time the Holy One, blessed be He, told Moses the matter of the sabbatical year and the Jubilee, to say that by the count of seven and by the count of forty-nine I grant this sanctity to the whole land of Israel, which bears resemblance and kinship with Mount Sinai, by virtue of its being an air that makes wise, and there is no Torah like the Torah of the land of Israel, and [like] Mount Sinai. Therefore it is fitting to give to that land as well the sanctity of Mount Sinai after a count of forty-nine years, and likewise by the count of seven — or, so as to make a remembrance of the standing at Mount Sinai through the proclamation of freedom and the sounding of the shofar, joined to the other reasons that this commandment has. Therefore 'on Mount Sinai' is stated. And this matter is sound and nearly to be heard."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The phrase 'וכו׳' (etc.) signals that the author is alluding to the well-known midrashic question (Torat Kohanim opening of Behar); rendered the ellipsis as 'and so forth.' The repeated word מ״ן in the phrase 'אוירא דמחכים' is left implicit as in the Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:20.799342856Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}